concept:australian-federal-police-and-other-legislation-amendment-act-2004-sch-1-sec-14m a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "14M Application to magistrate"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-1-sec-14m ;
    values:originalText "(1) If subsection 14L(7) applies, the police officer may apply to a magistrate for an order in relation to the thing to which that subsection applies.  (2) The magistrate must, in determining an application by a police officer under subsection (1), allow the owner of the thing to appear and be heard.  (3) If the magistrate is satisfied that:  (a) in the case of a thing seized under subsection 14K(1)—there are reasonable grounds to suspect that, if the thing is returned to the owner, the thing is likely to cause, or is likely to be used by the owner or another to cause:  (i) substantial damage to a place or thing in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions; or  (ii) death or serious harm to a person in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions;  in circumstances that would be likely to involve the commission of a protective service offence; or  (b) in the case of a thing seized under subsection 14K(2)—there are reasonable grounds to suspect that, if the thing is returned to the owner, the thing is likely to be used by the owner or another to cause death or serious harm to a person:  (i) in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions; or  (ii) who is in a place, or in the vicinity of a person, place or thing, in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions; the magistrate may make any of the following orders:  (c) that the thing be retained by the police officer for the period specified in the order;  (d) that the thing is forfeited to the Commonwealth;  (e) that the thing is to be sold and the proceeds given to the owner;  (f) that the thing is to be otherwise sold or disposed of.  Amendment of the Australian Federal Police Act 1979  Schedule 1  Australian Federal Police and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2004  (4) If the magistrate is not satisfied as mentioned in subsection (3), the magistrate must order that the thing be returned to the owner." ;
    skos:definition "(1) If subsection 14L(7) applies, the police officer may apply to a magistrate for an order in relation to the thing to which that subsection applies.  (2) The magistrate must, in determining an application by a police officer under subsection (1), allow the owner of the thing to appear and be heard.  (3) If the magistrate is satisfied that:  (a) in the case of a thing seized under subsection 14K(1)—there are reasonable grounds to suspect that, if the thing is returned to the owner, the thing is likely to cause, or is likely to be used by the owner or another to cause:  (i) substantial damage to a place or thing in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions; or  (ii) death or serious harm to a person in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions;  in circumstances that would be likely to involve the commission of a protective service offence; or  (b) in the case of a thing seized under subsection 14K(2)—there are reasonable grounds to suspect that, if the thing is returned to the owner, the thing is likely to be used by the owner or another to cause death or serious harm to a person:  (i) in respect of whom the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions; or  (ii) who is in a place, or in the vicinity of a person, place or thing, in respect of which the Australian Federal Police is performing protective service functions; the magistrate may make any of the following orders:  (c) that the thing be retained by the police officer for the period specified in the order;  (d) that the thing is forfeited to the Commonwealth;  (e) that the thing is to be sold and the proceeds given to the owner;  (f) that the thing is to be otherwise sold or disposed of.  Amendment of the Australian Federal Police Act 1979  Schedule 1  Australian Federal Police and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2004  (4) If the magistrate is not satisfied as mentioned in subsection (3), th"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "3cc14c9187db169f34f41c655b49101fe55f713833e39c93a7d74a6d73ccdf7c" ;
    cof:pageNumber "26"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-and-other-legislation-amendment-act-2004-sch-1-sec-14m-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-and-other-legislation-amendment-act-2004-sch-1-sec-14m-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-and-other-legislation-amendment-act-2004-sch-1-sec-14m-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-and-other-legislation-amendment-act-2004-sch-1-sec-14m-ss-4 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <australian-federal-police-and-other-legislation-amendment-act-2004.pdf> ;
